## THE DESIGN (Tuesday's defaults; every departure needs your written justification in STATUS)
**Three product-and-test commits, no product JavaScript.** The frozen program `apply1911.py` below edits `static/css/index-styles.css` (five anchored edits: three colour lines, the consumable comment, the consumable rule) and edits `static/css/dark-mode.css` twice (one anchored line joins `.drop-text` to the dark muted list; one block appended with two rules). Each anchor is asserted by its match count, each file is rebuilt whole and `os.replace`d, the non-ASCII bytes of each file are proved equal before and after, the added text is ASCII. The drafter's run on a scratch copy: `applied` for both files, `index-styles.css` 41678 to 42608 bytes, `dark-mode.css` 85161 to 85781, and a `diff` of the two pairs is the hunks listed in the sidecar (diff line classes: `index-styles.css` 18 `>` and 7 `<`, `dark-mode.css` 13 `>` and 0 `<`).
- **A** (`static/css/index-styles.css` and `static/css/dark-mode.css`): by `apply1911.py`.
- **F** (`__tests__/fixtures/index-light-failure-baseline.json`): by `apply1911_fixture.py` below.
- **T** (one new file): `__tests__/rd349-rd175-readable-text-on-its-ground.test.js`, 15 cells, extracted from this kit by `awk`.
- **K**: the counts file alone.
**Tuesday's defaults, said so they can be overruled (the product owner's ruling fixes the outcome, not these):** (1) the three grey texts take `#00719f` literally, as the RD-251 ruling did for the tab strip; (2) the toner label becomes an opaque chip, not a shadow or a colour change; (3) the dark track becomes `#313131`; (4) `.drop-text` joins the existing dark muted list (`#8c8c8c`, a token); (5) the e2e suite and its baselines are not edited (finding 7). If Tuesday overrules (1) the change is the one literal `TEXT` in `apply1911.py` and in the test file (`const TEXT`), plus the three ratio annotations the program recomputes; the seat does nothing about it by itself.

**The frozen program** (`apply1911.py`; run in wt, argument = wt's root; prints two `applied` lines; then each stylesheet is checked for balanced braces by the seat's own count and `git diff --stat` reads `index-styles.css | 25 ... `-style numbers recorded in STATUS (the drafter's, by `diff` line classes: 18 and 7 for index-styles.css, 13 and 0 for dark-mode.css):
```python FILE: apply1911.py
#!/usr/bin/env python3
# apply1911.py <tree root>: the frozen edit of lane N-L (RD-349, RD-175). ASCII-only added text.
# Every anchor is asserted to match the stated number of times; each file is rebuilt whole and os.replace'd;
# the non-ASCII bytes of each file are proved equal before and after.
import os, sys
root = sys.argv[1]
TEXT = '#00719f'

def rebuild(rel, edits, append=''):
    p = os.path.join(root, rel)
    raw = open(p, 'rb').read()
    s = raw.decode('utf-8')
    for name, old, new, n in edits:
        c = s.count(old)
        assert c == n, (rel, name, 'anchor count', c, 'expected', n)
        s = s.replace(old, new)
    s += append
    out = s.encode('utf-8')
    nonascii_before = [b for b in raw if b > 127]
    nonascii_after = [b for b in out if b > 127]
    assert nonascii_before == nonascii_after, (rel, 'non-ASCII bytes changed')
    tmp = p + '.tmp1911'
    open(tmp, 'wb').write(out)
    os.replace(tmp, p)
    print('applied', rel, len(raw), '->', len(out))

LIGHT_EDITS = [
 ('E1 metric-label', " .metric-card .metric-label {\n font-weight: 600;\n color: #7f8c8d;\n",
  " /* RD-349: the KPI labels were #7f8c8d on the white card, a failing grey that no static sweep can see because\n"
  "    static/js/index.js creates them. They take the brand chrome text colour that .tab-button took in RD-251,\n"
  "    measured on the ground they sit on (the card). Dark mode keeps its own rule in dark-mode.css. */\n"
  " .metric-card .metric-label {\n font-weight: 600;\n color: " + TEXT + "; /* @R1@:1 on #ffffff */\n", 1),
 ('E2 file-description', " .file-description {\n color: #7f8c8d;\n",
  " /* RD-349: same change; the ground is .import-section, #f8f9fa. Dark keeps its own rule. */\n"
  " .file-description {\n color: " + TEXT + "; /* @R2@:1 on #f8f9fa */\n", 1),
 ('E3 drop-text', " .drop-text {\n color: #7f8c8d;\n",
  " /* RD-349: same change; the ground is .file-drop-zone, white. Dark mode needs its own rule (dark-mode.css): the\n"
  "    colour below fails on the dark panel ground. */\n"
  " .drop-text {\n color: " + TEXT + "; /* @R3@:1 on #ffffff */\n", 1),
 ('E4 consumable comment', "    child \u2014 a narrow fill must never clip the label, RD-85). The outline\n    shadow keeps white text legible over the light #e9ecef track. */\n",
  "    child \u2014 a narrow fill must never clip the label, RD-85). RD-175: the label\n"
  "    carries a ground of its own. White text was 1.19:1 on the light #e9ecef track and the outline\n"
  "    text-shadow that stood in for contrast is not credited by WCAG; no single text colour clears the\n"
  "    track AND the four fills, so the label is a small opaque chip and the text-shadow is removed. */\n", 1),
 ('E5 consumable rule', " font-size: 0.8rem;\n color: white;\n text-shadow: 0 0 3px rgba(0,0,0,0.85), 0 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,0.6);\n white-space: nowrap;\n",
  " font-size: 0.8rem;\n color: #212529; /* @R4@:1 on #ffffff */\n background: white;\n padding: 0 6px;\n border-radius: 8px;\n line-height: 1.25;\n white-space: nowrap;\n", 1),
]
DARK_APPEND = (
 "\n/* RD-349 / RD-175: dark counterparts of the light changes in index-styles.css.\n"
 " * .drop-text: the light colour is now the brand chrome text colour, which fails on the dark panel ground, so it joins\n"
 " * the dark muted-text list above (.file-description). .consumable-bar / .consumable-text: the light\n"
 " * track #e9ecef does not belong on a dark page, and the label is a chip with a dark ground of its own. */\n"
 "body.dark-mode .consumable-bar {\n    background: #313131;\n}\n"
 "body.dark-mode .consumable-text {\n    color: #dedede;                   /* @R5@:1 on #1c1c1c */\n    background: #1c1c1c;\n}\n"
)
# the ratios are computed here by the repo's own formula (sRGB, WCAG 2.x) and written to three decimals
def lin(c):
    c /= 255.0
    return c / 12.92 if c <= 0.03928 else ((c + 0.055) / 1.055) ** 2.4
def lum(h):
    h = h.lstrip('#'); r, g, b = [int(h[i:i+2], 16) for i in (0, 2, 4)]
    return 0.2126 * lin(r) + 0.7152 * lin(g) + 0.0722 * lin(b)
def cr(a, b):
    x, y = lum(a), lum(b)
    if x < y: x, y = y, x
    return (x + 0.05) / (y + 0.05)
R = {'@R1@': cr(TEXT, '#ffffff'), '@R2@': cr(TEXT, '#f8f9fa'), '@R3@': cr(TEXT, '#ffffff'),
     '@R4@': cr('#212529', '#ffffff'), '@R5@': cr('#dedede', '#1c1c1c')}
def fill(s):
    for k, v in R.items(): s = s.replace(k, '%.3f' % v)
    return s
rebuild('static/css/index-styles.css', [(a, fill(b), fill(c), n) for a, b, c, n in LIGHT_EDITS])
rebuild('static/css/dark-mode.css', [('E6 drop-text joins the dark muted list', 'body.dark-mode .file-description,\nbody.dark-mode .s-2d7970a1c7,', 'body.dark-mode .file-description,\nbody.dark-mode .drop-text,\nbody.dark-mode .s-2d7970a1c7,', 1)], append=fill(DARK_APPEND))
```

## THE NEW TEST FILE (15 cells written as explicit `test(` calls; cell ids are the title prefixes; the colours at BASE are MEASURED-BY-DRAFTER in real jest 29.7.0 on a scratch copy of BASE with the file copied in untracked, and the green at head likewise)
**One frozen file.** Extract it with `awk` (the line after the opening fence to the line before the closing fence), `cmp` to what you extracted, never retype, never change a title (HOLD 38). It needs no server and no browser (`@jest-environment node`; it reads the two stylesheets, `static/js/index.js` and `static/index.html` as text and measures with the repo's own `contrastRatio`). It proves what the RULES say, not what a browser paints.
